<script lang="ts">
  export let onClose:()=>void;
  function open(node:HTMLDialogElement){node.showModal();return{destroy(){node.close()}}}
</script>
<dialog use:open onclose={onClose} onclick={(event)=>{if(event.target===event.currentTarget)onClose();}} aria-label="Analysis FAQ">
  <header><h2>Analysis FAQ</h2><button aria-label="Close FAQ" onclick={onClose}>×</button></header>
  <div class="faq-body">
    <details><summary>What does simulated speed show?</summary><p>The dashed line is the middle simulated free-running speed. It includes acceleration, section speed, skills, debuffs, slopes and supported statuses, and leaves traffic out. The shaded band is the low-to-high spread when a wit roll, pace mode, or downhill mode is still unknown. This white or black line is reserved and is not a runner color. Final-spurt targets use the HP budget and supported speed plateaus; they are not averaged with cruising speed when maximum spurt is supported.</p><p>Skill gains compare the same model with and without that activation. Recorded positions, HP and other runners stay fixed. This is a local comparison, not a prediction of a different race finish.</p></details>
    <details open><summary>What does utilization mean?</summary><p>Target-speed skills score 0% while blocked or below normal speed, 50% while accelerating from normal speed to the boosted target, and 100% at that target. Speed retained from the skill while slowing down also gets full credit. We compare paths with and without that skill, so speed left over from an unrelated effect does not count twice.</p><p>Normal speed includes other skills and debuffs. This score is separate from distance gained. Acceleration is useful while speeding up toward the cap; time at the cap is unused. Mixed skills show each effect.</p><p>For recoveries, HP efficiency shows how much HP was restored after the HP cap. It does not measure the recovery’s whole race benefit.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why does recovery timing matter?</summary><p>A recovery can restore all its HP and still arrive after a delayed or slower final spurt. Late recoveries can change the spurt plan, but the replay does not report each internal speed choice.</p><p>The max-spurt HP budget compares HP before and after recovery with a calculated cost to 60 m before the finish. It includes uphill slopes but leaves out acceleration, future skills and random modes. Covering the budget does not prove the game selected that speed.</p><p>Recovery details show the timing, HP waste and nearby speed changes. Those speed changes can include other skills, hills or blocking; they are not all caused by the recovery.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is speed carryover?</summary><p>Speed effects can leave extra speed that helps through a later acceleration or slowdown. That extra distance is carryover. Acceleration skills’ full head start counts as direct gain, even after their timer ends.</p><p>Carryover is included once in total gain. Its percentage uses speed potential: full speed bonus × duration. A 0.5 m/s bonus lasting 4 seconds has 2 m of potential; 0.4 m of carryover adds 20%. Mixed skills use only their speed portion. Acceleration has no carryover percentage.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How is skill duration calculated?</summary><p>Base duration × race distance ÷ 1000, with special duration modifiers applied when known. A 3-second skill lasts 7.5 seconds in a 2500 m race before other modifiers. Skill level changes effect strength, not this base duration.</p><p>Replay timing takes priority. Recoveries and other instant effects happen once. Their replay badges stay visible briefly so you can see them; that is not an active duration.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How does wit affect section speed?</summary><p>The race has 24 equal-distance sections. Each has a random target-speed adjustment based on starting race wit. These adjustments stop applying during the selected final spurt.</p><p>Avg. section speed shows the expected adjustment, not a measured gain. The replay does not report the individual rolls. Skills, acceleration and blocking can prevent the runner from reaching that target.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why is a result partial or unavailable?</summary><p>The replay records what happened, but not every internal speed target. Our model also cannot yet resolve every early position-keeping mode, blocking transition or overlapping effect.</p><p>A range includes unknown time. For example, 83–100% use means 83% was useful and the other 17% is unknown. The striped part of the bar shows that uncertainty. It does not mean the replay is damaged or the skill failed.</p><p>Skill details show the reason. Unknown time is kept separate from time spent at the speed cap or after the finish.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Are distance and time gains exact?</summary><p>They compare the replay with a calculation that removes one effect while keeping the other known effects fixed. Time saved is the travel-time equivalent of the distance gain, not a prediction of the final result.</p><p>Overlapping skill and status gains should not be added together.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are hills calculated?</summary><p>Uphill target-speed loss is slope × 200 ÷ power, with slope expressed as a percentage. More power reduces the loss. The power comparison uses 1000 power.</p><p>Downhill mode adds 0.3 + |slope| ÷ 10 m/s and uses 40% of normal HP drain. Being on a downhill section alone does not mean downhill mode is active.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are outer-corner losses calculated?</summary><p>We compare the runner’s path with lane 0 around each corner. Extra distance comes from the recorded lane and the game course’s turning angle. Equivalent time uses the runner’s recorded speed.</p><p>The calculation uses 11.25 metres per lane unit, supported by game constants and the reference mechanics. It excludes weaving and changes to race decisions, so it is not an exact finish-time loss.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does the Rushed bonus show?</summary><p>Rushed can raise the target by 4% of base strategy speed. The distance is how much of that extra speed she actually ran. Time spent blocked, or still below the ordinary target, adds nothing. While she is climbing onto the 4%, only the surplus already reached counts. An unknown spurt target leaves the window unavailable.</p><p>Extra HP compares the same speed trace with the rushed drain removed. Unknown required inputs leave the result unavailable.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does the replay report downhill and position-keep modes?</summary><p>This replay format reports position, speed, HP, rushed state and front blocking. It does not directly report DH, PU, PD, SU or overtake mode.</p><p>DH is inferred from reduced HP drain on a downhill, after accounting for recoveries and debuffs. Position-keep modes use skill-adjusted speed, strategy and gaps. Their distance and HP gains compare paths with and without the mode, including acceleration and traffic. Unknown intervals stay uncertain; no detected mode does not prove the mode was off.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How are blocking and exhaustion losses calculated?</summary><p>Traffic loss compares recorded speed with a free-running path starting at the same speed before the slowdown. It includes braking before a recorded block and the time needed to regain speed. Being nearby alone is not enough: the front distance and lane gap must fit the collision rule.</p><p>Exhaustion compares the recorded run with one that still has HP. Ranges cover unknown speed targets; partial results cover only supported time. These losses can overlap, so adding them together can count the same lost distance twice.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How do you check HP debuffs?</summary><p>We compare the recorded HP drop with normal running drain, then with running drain plus the debuff. Speed, ground, race phase and supported drain modifiers affect the comparison.</p><p>A hit is supported when only the damage calculation fits. Immunity is supported when only normal drain fits. If both fit, or the inputs are missing, the result stays unknown. This does not assign teams.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why didn’t a skill activate?</summary><p>We check its conditions across the race. A red × means no activation was found. Failed conditions and wit failures are shown separately. An inferred wit failure needs a complete recording and a guaranteed chance to activate under the model.</p><p>All condition rules in the bundled Global skill data have been reviewed. Some replays still lack team, NPC or random-trigger data needed to decide the result. Knowing a rule does not recover those missing inputs.</p><p>Guaranteed passives count as active when their conditions are met, even if the replay does not list a separate activation. Some starting acceleration skills can also be inferred when their initial marker, conditions and recorded acceleration agree. These are labeled as inferred.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What do the replay effects show?</summary><p>Skill bars show time remaining. Colored badges mark active statuses. Debuff badges identify recipients listed in the replay.</p><p>Vision cones show the skill’s range. A runner inside a cone is a possible target; a recorded hit confirms the target.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which game version do the calculations use?</summary><p>Steam Global. Mechanics from later game updates are added only when supported for this version.</p></details>
  </div>
</dialog>
